{"kind": "instrument", "q": "Q2", "file": "crates/memra-engine/cu/qmatvec.cu:3258-3300 (qmatvec_e4m3_mmvq / e4m3_row_dot) \u2014 ALREADY IN TREE, ships behind MEMRA_ST_E4M3=1", "tree_check_result": "NO new kernel was needed. The native-e4m3 m=1 GEMV already exists, reads raw checkpoint e4m3 bytes as its weight stream (row_bytes == in_f, no dequant), and is already correctness-gated at m=1 by kernel-check (crates/.../kernel_check.rs:2181-2270: f64 CPU e4m3 reference + grid.y=m and b2/b4/b8 bit-parity arms, for mm in [1,2,5,9]). What was missing \u2014 and all this lane added \u2014 is the A/B PERF measurement; the kernel shipped with end-to-end evidence only.", "bench": "crates/memra-engine/src/bin/gemv_e4m3_bench.rs", "arms": {"E4M3": "QT_F8_E4M3, row_bytes=in_f -> out_f*in_f bytes", "Q8_0": "QT_Q8_0, row_bytes=(in_f/32)*34 -> out_f*(in_f/32)*34 bytes"}, "same_everything_else": "both arms ride qmatvec_mmvq_raw, which quantizes the SAME f32 x to q8_1 and launches the warp-per-row MMVQ for the given qtype", "dram_cold": "each shape allocates `copies` independent weight buffers (768 MB rotation set / weight bytes, clamped 1..64) and both arms rotate identically, so consecutive launches never re-read L2-resident bytes", "not_the_same_arithmetic": "Q8_0 does 8 dp4a into s32 per 32-block; e4m3 does 8 cvt + 16 fmaf in f32. This measures TIME; kernel-check owns the e4m3 GEMV numeric gate, and container-level model equivalence is v2's teacher-forced + NLL protocol.", "byte_ceiling": "34/32 = 1.0625x => +6.25pp if perfectly bandwidth-bound"}
